---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import SidebarNav from '../components/SidebarNav.astro';
import BottomNav from '../components/BottomNav.astro';
import MainContent from '../components/MainContent.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import SFTDemo from '../components/SFTDemo.astro';
import NextSteps from '../components/NextSteps.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Supervised Fine-Tuning — AI Concepts Visualized"
  description="How teaching a model to follow instructions transforms a base model into a helpful assistant."
>
  <SidebarNav activeSlug="sft" />
  <BottomNav activeTab="training" />
  <MainContent>
    <Breadcrumbs items={[
      { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
      { label: 'Training Stages', href: '/training-stages' },
      { label: 'Supervised Fine-Tuning' },
    ]} />

    <!-- Hero -->
    <header class="mb-12">
      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-4">
        <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-coral" />
        <span class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400">Stage 2 of 3</span>
      </span>
      <h1 class="text-2xl font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-3">
        Supervised Fine-Tuning
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-prose mb-4">
        Imagine giving the model a teacher. Where pre-training had the model learn from everything
        on the internet, SFT pairs it with a curated set of examples showing exactly how to respond
        to instructions — turning a knowledgeable model into a helpful one.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-coral">
        <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <path d="M4 6h16M4 12h10M4 18h13" />
        </svg>
        <span class="font-medium">Giving the model a teacher</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Interactive demo -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <SFTDemo />
    </section>

    <!-- What is SFT? -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
        What Is Supervised Fine-Tuning?
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) is the training stage that sits between pre-training and
        preference tuning. It takes a base model that has learned language patterns from vast
        corpora and teaches it to follow instructions by training on carefully curated
        instruction-response pairs.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The key insight is simple: if you show a model thousands of examples of good
        instruction-following behavior, it learns to replicate that pattern. Instead of just
        predicting the next token in a Wikipedia article, the model learns to recognize when
        someone is asking a question, requesting a summary, or asking for creative writing —
        and responds appropriately.
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-coral/20 bg-coral/6 p-5">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-coral">The core idea:</strong> Pre-training teaches the model <em>what</em> to say
          (knowledge). SFT teaches the model <em>how</em> to say it (behavior).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Why SFT matters -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
        Why SFT Matters
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Without SFT, a pre-trained model is like a brilliant but directionless scholar — it knows
        a tremendous amount but doesn't know how to be useful. SFT bridges this gap.
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
            <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-md bg-coral/10 flex items-center justify-center">
              <span class="text-sm font-mono font-bold text-coral">1</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">Instruction Following</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
            The model learns to recognize and respond to different types of instructions —
            questions, commands, creative requests — rather than just continuing text.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
            <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-md bg-coral/10 flex items-center justify-center">
              <span class="text-sm font-mono font-bold text-coral">2</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">Response Format</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
            SFT teaches structured output: when to use lists, when to write paragraphs, when to
            provide code blocks, and how to match the expected format.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
            <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-md bg-coral/10 flex items-center justify-center">
              <span class="text-sm font-mono font-bold text-coral">3</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">Tone &amp; Style</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
            The model learns to adopt a helpful, informative tone rather than generating text
            in whatever style it encountered during pre-training.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
            <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-md bg-coral/10 flex items-center justify-center">
              <span class="text-sm font-mono font-bold text-coral">4</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">Task Adaptation</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
            Domain-specific SFT can specialize a general model for tasks like coding, medical
            diagnosis, or legal analysis by training on expert-curated examples.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How SFT differs from pre-training -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
        How SFT Differs From Pre-training
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Both stages use the same underlying mechanism — updating weights by minimizing prediction
        loss. But everything else about the two stages is fundamentally different.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 bg-gray-50">
              <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-xs text-gray-400">
                Aspect
              </th>
              <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-medium text-coral">
                Pre-training
              </th>
              <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-medium text-coral">
                SFT
              </th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400">Data</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Trillions of raw tokens from the internet</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Thousands of curated instruction-response pairs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400">Goal</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Learn language patterns &amp; world knowledge</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Learn to follow instructions</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400">Scale</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Massive — months on thousands of GPUs</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Modest — hours to days on a single GPU</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400">Data quality</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Filtered but still noisy and diverse</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Human-written or carefully verified</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400">Output</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Base model — knowledgeable but unhelpful</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Instruct model — helpful but misaligned</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The SFT training process -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
        The SFT Training Process
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-coral/10 border border-coral/30 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-coral">1</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-0.5">Collect Seed Data</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
              Start with a small dataset of high-quality instruction-response pairs. These may be
              written by human annotators, synthesized from the base model itself, or extracted
              from existing QA datasets.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-coral/10 border border-coral/30 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-coral">2</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-0.5">Format as Training Examples</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
              Each example is formatted as a prompt-response pair, often with special tokens
              marking the instruction and response boundaries (e.g., "### Instruction:" and
              "### Response:"). The model trains to predict the response given the instruction.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-coral/10 border border-coral/30 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-coral">3</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-0.5">Train on Response Tokens</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
              The loss is computed only on the response tokens — the model learns to generate the
              correct response given the instruction, without being penalized for "predicting" the
              instruction itself (which is given as input).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-coral/10 border border-coral/30 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-coral">4</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-0.5">Iterate &amp; Expand</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
              Early SFT rounds may use model-generated data to expand the dataset. Subsequent
              rounds refine the model further. This self-instruct loop helps cover edge cases and
              improves overall instruction-following capability.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Key considerations -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
        Key Considerations
      </h2>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5 mb-4">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
          <span class="text-coral mr-2">⚠</span>Catastrophic Forgetting
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Aggressive fine-tuning can cause the model to forget knowledge learned during
          pre-training. The trick is tuning enough to gain instruction-following ability while
          preserving the broad knowledge base. Techniques like low-rank adaptation (LoRA) help
          mitigate this risk.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5 mb-4">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
          <span class="text-coral mr-2">⚠</span>Data Quality Over Quantity
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          SFT is sensitive to data quality. A small dataset of excellent examples often produces
          better results than a large dataset of mediocre ones. The style and quality of the
          training data directly shapes the model's output style.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
          <span class="text-coral mr-2">⚠</span>SFT Is Not Enough
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          SFT makes a model helpful, but not necessarily aligned with human values. A model can
          follow instructions to do harmful things. The next stage — preference tuning (RLHF or
          DPO) — addresses safety and value alignment.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <NextSteps
      accentColor="var(--color-coral)"
      items={[
        { label: 'Pre-training', href: '/pre-training', description: 'The foundation stage' },
        { label: 'Preference Tuning', href: '/preference-tuning', description: 'Aligning to human values' },
      ]}
    />
  </MainContent>
</Layout>
